{
  "node_id": "a2ce8d89-ef74-405e-a417-4d1d11036f4d",
  "level": 0,
  "summary": "The second morning of his stay in the house Salve had perceived that\nthere was a want of money; and having heard the brother and sister\nquarrelling one day when both were in a bad humour, he thought it best\nto carry out, at the first convenient moment, the determination at which\nhe had arrived, and handed over to Federigo what money he had, with the\nexception of a single silver piastre, saying, \"That it was only right he\nshould pay for his lodging and board. When the time came for breaking up, Federigo was drunk, and Salve was\nobliged to accompany his inconsolable friend in the darkness over the\nlong narrow dam down by the dock, where there was water on both sides,\nFederigo clinging to his arm the whole way, and leaning heavily upon it.",
